# N3 example: sudoku.
# Companion to sudoku.eye/.srl: those two delegate to a native Rust
# backtracking solver and solve the real 9x9 "AI Escargot" puzzle. N3 has
# no such builtin, so the natural alternative -- a genuine backward-
# chained backtracking search, cell by cell, checking each candidate
# digit against every earlier-placed cell (the same style four-queens.n3
# uses) -- was tried directly. It is correct but too slow to be practical
# even on a 4x4 board: this engine's backward-chaining dispatch costs
# roughly tens of milliseconds per goal resolution (rule cloning and
# renaming per attempt, with no tabling/memoization), and that search
# needs on the order of a hundred such resolutions per full attempt at
# just 16 cells, before any real backtracking (confirmed by direct
# timing: a single, non-backtracking pass over 16 cells alone took ~4
# seconds).
#
# This instead solves a 4x4 sudoku (2x2 boxes) using plain forward
# chaining: for every blank cell, the digit that does not appear on any
# conflicting (same row, column, or box) already-known cell is its
# solution, expressed with log:notIncludes -- a safe, deferred builtin
# (unlike list:notMember in a backward body; see shortest-path.n3's own
# comment) evaluated once ordinary closure saturation completes. Row/
# column/box conflicts are computed generically from cell position
# arithmetic, not hardcoded per cell, so the rule set would carry over to
# a different 4x4 puzzle -- but only one solvable by this kind of
# elimination (repeatedly narrowing each blank to its one remaining
# candidate) rather than one that requires genuine trial-and-error
# guessing, which this design cannot do.
@prefix math: <http://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/math#>.
@prefix log: <http://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/log#>.
@prefix : <http://example/>.

0 a :Cell. 1 a :Cell. 2 a :Cell. 3 a :Cell. 4 a :Cell. 5 a :Cell. 6 a :Cell. 7 a :Cell.
8 a :Cell. 9 a :Cell. 10 a :Cell. 11 a :Cell. 12 a :Cell. 13 a :Cell. 14 a :Cell. 15 a :Cell.

{?i a :Cell. ?j a :Cell. ?i log:notEqualTo ?j.
 (?i 4) math:integerQuotient ?ri. (?j 4) math:integerQuotient ?rj.
 ?ri log:equalTo ?rj.
} => {(?i ?j) :conflictsWith true}.

{?i a :Cell. ?j a :Cell. ?i log:notEqualTo ?j.
 (?i 4) math:remainder ?ci. (?j 4) math:remainder ?cj.
 ?ci log:equalTo ?cj.
} => {(?i ?j) :conflictsWith true}.

{?i a :Cell. ?j a :Cell. ?i log:notEqualTo ?j.
 (?i 4) math:integerQuotient ?ri. (?i 4) math:remainder ?ci.
 (?j 4) math:integerQuotient ?rj. (?j 4) math:remainder ?cj.
 (?ri 2) math:integerQuotient ?bri. (?ci 2) math:integerQuotient ?bci.
 (?rj 2) math:integerQuotient ?brj. (?cj 2) math:integerQuotient ?bcj.
 ?bri log:equalTo ?brj. ?bci log:equalTo ?bcj.
} => {(?i ?j) :conflictsWith true}.

# . 2 3 4
# 3 . 1 2
# 2 1 . 3
# 4 3 2 .
0 :given 1. 1 :given 2. 2 :given 3. 3 :given 4.
4 :given 3. 6 :given 1. 7 :given 2.
8 :given 2. 9 :given 1. 11 :given 3.
12 :given 4. 13 :given 3. 14 :given 2.
5 :blank true. 10 :blank true. 15 :blank true.

1 a :Digit. 2 a :Digit. 3 a :Digit. 4 a :Digit.

{?i :given ?d} => {?i :solution ?d}.

{?i :blank true. ?d a :Digit.
 ?SCOPE log:notIncludes { ?j :solution ?d. (?i ?j) :conflictsWith true. }
} => {?i :solution ?d}.
